// Practice quiz: Health checks
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/integration-and-observability/health-checks

::o1 default view::What does a default /actuator/health show? {
~Every component#Not quite. That needs show-components.
=Only the aggregate status#Correct. Components and details default to never.
~Database credentials#Not quite. It never shows those.
~The source code#Not quite. It does not.
}

::o1 db contributor::What does the db health contributor check? {
=That a connection to the DataSource can be obtained#Correct. It reports its status under components.
~That all rows are valid#Not quite. It does not read your data.
~That migrations are newest#Not quite. That is not this check.
~Query speed#Not quite. It does not time your queries.
}

::o2 exposure::By default, which Actuator endpoint is exposed over HTTP? {
~env#Not quite. It could reveal configuration.
~heapdump#Not quite. It could reveal memory contents.
=health#Correct. The others stay unexposed until you add them.
~loggers#Not quite. It can change log levels.
}

::o3 liveness::What does a failing liveness probe usually cause? {
~The process stops getting traffic only#Not quite. That is readiness.
~A database backup#Not quite. It is unrelated.
~A code deploy#Not quite. It is unrelated.
=The platform restarts the process#Correct. Liveness means the process should be restarted if unhealthy.
}

::o3 readiness::What does a failing readiness probe usually cause? {
~The process is restarted#Not quite. That is liveness.
~The JVM crashes#Not quite. A probe does not do that.
~Logging turns off#Not quite. It is unrelated.
=The platform stops routing traffic to it#Correct. It can recover without a restart.
}

